{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 posits a causal mechanism (school-0 demand) by splitting failure modes: unified codebase causes logic corruption to cascade across all centralized nodes, while diverse codebases in decentralized services prevent total failure. However, this mechanism does not address what evidence distinguishes failures that are truly independent from those that merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found, a core requirement of the problem. The two counterconditions (cellular architecture, undersized services) are asserted without derivation or observation, leaving the central problem context unmet.","target":"52769d90cc6684e6a3bf18fbd1dc434cc05605781119d94394d16114faaed92b"},{"attack":false,"case":"SRC_002 proposes a substantive contradiction between the Poisson independence claim and pseudo-correlation from cascading effects, and names a valid refutation condition (trigger-based synchronization without shared external cause). This satisfies the demand for a causal mechanism (the cascade mechanism mimics correlation) and the relation-form refuted-if clause.","target":"95a76bd6d9c33200069ea10f7189cc0dfdeeca43b7088a9cb608d98de28a1b00"},{"attack":false,"case":"SRC_003 proposes a substantive abstraction relation (mapping specific triggers into a general class of correlated failures) and gives a concrete refutation condition (single-timestamp mapping with MTTR-proportional recovery decoupling trigger from recovery). This satisfies the relation requirement and the causal-mechanism stance by identifying what the abstraction depends on and how it breaks.","target":"a516968e5478ca9d557859ea8601129d71fd03ed74f04deda6038460b09890db"},{"attack":true,"case":"TARGET SRC_004 is empty (only the header 'T' appears). It provides no claim, mechanism, relation, or evidence regarding decentralized vs centralized resilience or distinguishing independent from apparent uncorrelated failures, failing the problem context entirely.","target":"f4a43d98e850